<div class="glossary-section">
    <h2>Conceptual Glossary</h2>
    <div class="glossary-grid">
        <details>
            <summary>Quantity-Quality (Q-Q) Trade-off</summary>
            <div class="details-content">
                <p>A microeconomic framework suggesting that as income rises or the "price" of child quality (education) falls, parents choose to invest more in fewer children rather than having a larger family.</p>
            </div>
        </details>
        
        <details>
            <summary>Exogenous Shock</summary>
            <div class="details-content">
                <p>An external event (like the 1833 Factory Act) that changes the environment for participants but is not caused by the participants themselves. This is crucial for establishing causality in econometrics.</p>
            </div>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Total Fertility Rate (TFR)</summary>
            <div class="details-content">
                <p>The average number of children that would be born to a woman over her lifetime if she were to experience the exact current age-specific fertility rates through her reproductive life.</p>
            </div>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Difference-in-Differences (DiD)</summary>
            <div class="details-content">
                <p>A statistical technique that calculates the effect of a treatment by comparing the change in outcomes over time between a treated group and a control group.</p>
            </div>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Oster's Delta (δ)</summary>
            <div class="details-content">
                <p>A robustness metric developed by Emily Oster. It quantifies how strong unobserved selection would need to be relative to observed selection to explain away an estimated effect.</p>
            </div>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Spatial Weights</summary>
            <div class="details-content">
                <p>A method to account for the geographic proximity of observations. It acknowledges that districts close to each other are likely to share similar trends due to regional economic spills.</p>
            </div>
        </details>
    </div>
</div>

<style>
    .glossary-section {
        margin-top: 6rem;
        background: var(--secondary);
        padding: 4rem;
        border-radius: 8px;
        border: 1px solid rgba(212, 175, 55, 0.1);
    }
    h2 {
        font-family: var(--serif);
        color: var(--accent);
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
        text-align: center;
    }
    .glossary-grid {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
        gap: 1.5rem;
    }
    details {
        border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);
        padding: 1rem 0;
        cursor: pointer;
    }
    summary {
        font-weight: bold;
        color: var(--accent);
        font-family: var(--sans);
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.1rem;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        list-style: none;
        display: flex;
        justify-content: space-between;
        align-items: center;
    }
    summary::after {
        content: '+';
        font-size: 1.2rem;
        transition: transform 0.3s;
    }
    details[open] summary::after {
        transform: rotate(45deg);
    }
    .details-content {
        padding-top: 1rem;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        opacity: 0.8;
        line-height: 1.6;
    }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {
        .glossary-grid {
            grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        }
    }
</style>
